 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "THE PEACE BRIDGE. A curving footbridge opened in 2011, 235 metres long, joining the two sides of the Foyle in one graceful S. It is made for walkers and cyclists, and for the sort of person who likes to cross a river in a flourish."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE DIAMOND. The market square at the middle of the walled city, a town square with the streets running off the four sides like the arms of a clock. The pigeons hold their meetings here at noon."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "THE WALLS. Built between 1613 and 1618, about 1.5 kilometres round, and still whole: Derry is the only city in Ireland that still has its walls all the way round. You can walk the whole top. It takes about an hour, or two with the views."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "ROARING MEG. A big old cannon on the walls that earned its name by being loud. It's been quiet for centuries, which in cannons counts as a hard-won retirement."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "BOGSIDE. West of the walls, the oak woods that gave the city its name: Doire means 'oak grove'. Many of the oaks are long gone, but plenty of people still say 'Derry' and mean every one of them."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "THE GUILDHALL. A big red Victorian building with a clock tower and a hall full of stained glass, built in 1890 and rebuilt after a fire in 1908. It has seen concerts, councils and one remarkable wedding cake."
  }
 ],
